<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # That's a Big Book! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Life Adventures</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Big Book!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write simple thank-you notes or greetings in cards using one-sentence picture cards. Interactive: Contribute a word to a class story or sentence, with help.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple, polite thank-you notes or greetings using familiar classroom vocabulary and adjectives (e.g., "Hello, friend! Thank you for the red pencil."). Can contribute words to a simple story describing classroom objects (e.g., "red," "green," and "pencil"). Can describe classroom objects in photos or drawings using adjectives and the present simple tense (e.g., "This is a big, blue book." or "The bag is small and brown.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Hello, everyone! How are you?" and leads a quick TPR activity. Students will point to classroom objects as the teacher calls them out with adjectives. For example: "Point to the big book!" "Show me the red pencil!" "Find the small bag!" The teacher then writes the words students identify (e.g., "book," "pencil," "bag," "red," "big," "small") on the board, emphasizing their written form and initial sounds like /b/ in "book" and /p/ in "pencil." Students repeat words chorally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a blue crayon. The teacher explicitly models how to write a simple sentence on the board: "This is a blue crayon." The teacher narrates the writing process: "First, I write 'This is a'. Then I add the color 'blue' and the object 'crayon'. Don't forget the period!" The teacher then models writing a thank-you note: "Thank you for the blue crayon." The teacher asks students to chorally repeat the phrases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'big' a color or a size word?" (Expected answer: "Size!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we finish writing a sentence, what do we put at the end?" (Expected answer: "A period!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "School Life Adventures"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large picture cards showing classroom objects (e.g., a red pencil, a big blue book, a small yellow bag) with corresponding simple thank-you notes or greetings written clearly below each picture (e.g., "Hello, friend!", "Thank you for the red pencil!", "This is a big book!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students can recognize and match written words for classroom objects and simple adjectives. They can understand the purpose of a simple greeting or thank-you note.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a picture card (e.g., a red pencil with "Thank you for the red pencil!"). Students are asked to point to the word "red" and the word "pencil."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher presents two written sentences (e.g., "This is a blue bag." and "Thank you for the big book."). The teacher holds up a picture of a big book and asks, "Which sentence matches the picture?" Students point to the correct sentence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify the greeting "Hello" and the polite expression "Thank you" on the cards by circling them with their fingers in the air as the teacher points.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing simple sentences using adjectives and the present simple tense (e.g., "This is a big, blue book."). Writing polite thank-you notes using familiar classroom vocabulary (e.g., "Thank you for the red pencil."). Grammar: Adjectives, Present simple. Vocabulary: book, pencil, bag, crayon, red, blue, big, small. Pragmatic: Describing objects with detail, employing polite expressions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Tracing Sentences:</b> The teacher provides worksheets with dotted-line sentences related to classroom objects and thank-you notes. Students trace sentences such as: "This is a big book." "Thank you for the red pencil." "Hello, friend!" The teacher circulates, offering help with letter formation and ensuring students say the words as they trace.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Fill-in-the-Blanks:</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with simple sentences and a word bank (e.g., "red," "blue," "big," "small," "book," "pencil," "bag"). Students complete sentences by filling in the blanks. Examples: "This is a ______ ______." (e.g., 'red', 'pencil') or "Thank you for the ______ ______." (e.g., 'small', 'bag'). The teacher guides students to choose appropriate words based on picture prompts next to each sentence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Sentence Construction - Class Story/Sentence:</b> The teacher holds up a classroom object (e.g., a small brown bag). The teacher asks, "What is this?" and "What color is it?" and "What size is it?" (WH-questions practice). Students contribute words (e.g., "small," "brown," "bag"). The teacher writes these words on the board to form a sentence: "This is a small, brown bag." The teacher then guides students to copy this sentence into their notebooks, practicing the complete sentence structure.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "School Life Adventures"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students will create simple thank-you notes or greeting cards for classroom objects, incorporating adjectives and polite expressions. They will draw a picture of a classroom item and write a short, one-sentence note.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher distributes blank card templates or small pieces of paper.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose one classroom object (e.g., a crayon, a book, a ruler, an eraser) they have used or seen in the "School Life Adventures" theme.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They draw a picture of their chosen object (e.g., a blue crayon, a big red book).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below their drawing, students write a simple thank-you note or greeting using the sentence frames modeled earlier. Examples: "Hello, friend! Thank you for the blue crayon." or "Thank you for the big, red book." The teacher provides the frames on the board for reference.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to use at least one adjective and a polite expression. The teacher reminds them to write clearly and put a period at the end of their sentence.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher's Role:</b> Circulate, provide individual support, check for correct spelling of familiar words and sentence structure, and encourage students to focus on clear communication in writing. Prompt with questions like, "What color is your crayon?" to elicit adjective use.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on written output and a simple peer assessment activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their written thank-you notes/cards with a partner. Each student will briefly "read" (identify words) their partner's note aloud to the teacher and point to the object and the adjective. The teacher observes if students correctly identified words and whether their own written note meets the criteria.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legible handwriting.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of 1-2 target vocabulary words (e.g., "book," "pencil," "bag," "crayon").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of at least one adjective (e.g., "red," "blue," "big," "small").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct simple sentence structure (e.g., "Thank you for the [adjective] [noun].").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear expression of thanks or greeting.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students will complete an exit ticket with the following prompts by drawing a happy face or a sad face:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write 'Thank you' in English."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write about a [color] [object] like 'red pencil'."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and Teacher reflection</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to write today in our thank-you notes?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing was a little difficult for me?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students write simple thank-you notes? Was the progression from tracing to guided writing effective? Most students were able to correctly write "Thank you for the [adjective] [noun]." The explicit modeling was crucial.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with letter formation for less familiar words or remembering the period. For the next lesson, I will provide more structured sentence starters or word banks directly on the project material, and incorporate a quick warm-up on punctuation.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their writing skills to create a collaborative classroom poster or mini-book about "That's a Big Book!", integrating the descriptions and polite expressions learned today into a larger project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>